<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Garðar, Greenland</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gardar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Garðar was the seat of the Norse <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenland">Greenland</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-settlement">Eastern Settlement</a> at the site of the modern village of Igaliku in southern Greenland. The first resident bishop, Arnald, arrived around 1126, and a cathedral dedicated to St Nicholas was built there beside a large farm. Bishops of Garðar continued to be appointed into the 16th century, though none lived there after the 14th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gardar-greenland.jpg" alt="Garðar, Greenland" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: jtstewart · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vatnahverfi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Garðar was the first Catholic diocese established in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. According to the sagas, Sokki Þórisson, a wealthy farmer of the Brattahlíð area, proposed a separate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenland">Greenland</a> early in the 12th century and won the approval of the Norwegian king Sigurd I Magnusson. Most of the clergy came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>. The diocese was first placed under the Archbishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a> and then under the Archdiocese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lund">Lund</a>, and in 1152 it became, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iceland">Iceland</a>, the Isle of Man, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faroe-islands">Faroe Islands</a>, a suffragan of the new Archdiocese of Nidaros.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Arnaldur, the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>, was ordained by the Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lund">Lund</a> in 1124 and began the cathedral; he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> in 1150 and became bishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamar">Hamar</a>. His successors included Jón Knútr, Jón Árnason, who went on pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1202–1203, met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iii">Pope Innocent III</a> and was buried at Garðar in 1209, and Ólafr, who was abroad from 1264 to 1280. Communication with Norway was so poor that Bishop Árni was presumed dead and Jón Skalli was ordained in 1343; Skalli resigned when Árni proved to be alive and never went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenland">Greenland</a>.</p><p>After Árni died in 1347 the see stood vacant for 19 years, while the Norwegian cleric Ivar Bardsson led the church. Álfr, ordained in 1365, was the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> to reside, until 1378, and when Björn Einarsson Jórsalafari landed in 1385 a priest was administering the diocese. It disappeared in the 15th century, when ships from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> stopped arriving. Bishops were still named to the see until 1537 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>–Norway, apparently without any of them visiting it. In 1996 Garðar was revived as a titular see.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The settlement of Igaliku stands on the site, which has been studied by archaeologists since the 1830s. The Danish archaeologist Poul Nørlund fully excavated the cathedral in 1926. Its ruin, the main one, is of a cross-shaped sandstone church of the 12th century, up to 27 metres long and 16 metres wide, and the stone foundations of many Norse buildings remain visible, among them two large barns that could have held up to 160 cows.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Garðar, Greenland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They held their own assembly, and from 1126 a bishop lived at <b>Garðar</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/norse-greenland">Norse Greenland</a></li></ul></section>
